      "writtenOn": "2020-09-11T08:53:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This change is mixing two pieces of functionality - adding structs and merging nested sets. I think that they should be split out into two separate changes:\n1. Transform struct into a BpPropertySet.\n2. Merging BpPropertySets together.\n\nThe current solution mixes those together which makes both parts more complicated than necessary. The logic for merging has to handle both structs and BpPropertySet. By having them as separate changes you can simplify each piece.\n\nI think that they will each need some unit tests, i.e. ones that don\u0027t require parsing a whole .bp file such as the existing ones in bp_test.go.",
      "revId": "e5bf895264f4551430e9e33b4d738d80d75559ff",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f3e1ad99_7f5a184b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-09-15T01:43:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "No, I tried that first. Handling it in two stages adds code to construct a full bpPropertySet rather than just merging the struct fields, duplicates a bit of type testing logic, and doesn\u0027t help with the messy logic handling new/existing values in AddProperty.\n\nSplitting out the merging code helps, which I\u0027ve done now. I\u0027ve also cleaned up some unnecessary complications trying to avoid adding empty sets.\n\nI\u0027ve split off the sanitizer and updater changes from the bp.go stuff and added unit tests, but there\u0027s not much value in separating out the property struct handling anymore since it\u0027s just an else clause in mergePropertySet now.",
      "parentUuid": "8f921509_bf01bf6e",
      "revId": "e5bf895264f4551430e9e33b4d738d80d75559ff",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1aa130f7_4671e0ce",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-09-15T14:38:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There are two orthogonal issues here.\n\nOne is best practice for git changes. Having a single functional change per git change makes it much easier to review and manage. Even if the follow up change modifies the same area of code as the first one. Also, I am not 100% convinced that merging property sets is required so I would like to see a concrete use case for it before we add it. Having it as a separate change means that the first part which you have a need for won\u0027t be delayed by the second part which you were a little unclear about.\n\nThe second issue is separation of concerns. While I agree that there is a similarity in pattern between merging in a BpPropertySet and converting a struct I don\u0027t think that justifies them being done in the same method.\n\nThe common pattern:\n* Recursing through the set/struct.\n* Checking types.\n\nHowever, the types that are handled are different. struct -\u003e BpPropertySet needs to handle fields and nested structs and slices but BpPropertySet merging needs to handle BpPropertySets. e.g. there are huge chunks of AddProperty() which are specific to struct -\u003e property set conversion.\n\nYou mentioned a mergePropertySet() method but I can\u0027t see it did you forget to upload a change?",
      "parentUuid": "f3e1ad99_7f5a184b",
      "revId": "e5bf895264f4551430e9e33b4d738d80d75559ff",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bc3ff3bc_2b6dbe03",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-09-15T16:15:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Also, I am not 100% convinced that merging property sets is required so I would like to see a concrete use case for it before we add it.\n\nI discussed this in an earlier response, but can add that concretely the line https://r.android.com/c/platform/build/soong/+/1427729/2/cc/library_sdk_member.go#224 would fail in some calls, and there\u0027s no way to query if a property already exists through the BpPropertySet interface (which in itself I think is fine).\n\nOne can very well add set.property_a in one call and set.property_b in another. Having to deal with checking for and adding the struct only once would mean the caller effectively has to traverse the sets/structs themselves, which defeats the point with this. And, as mentioned earler, the sets themselves don\u0027t carry any info, which is what makes a check against clobbering valuable.\n\n\u003e The second issue is separation of concerns.\n\nI sincerely appreciate that, and I took your suggested approach myself initially. But after a while I realised it gets overall simpler if type checking and handling of the nested property set already present (i.e. the potential merge target) is done first, and the type dispatch on the value is done afterwards.\n\nSince you apparently haven\u0027t seen mergePropertySet I\u0027ll leave it as-is for now. The separation between property sets and structs is straightforward there, so I don\u0027t think a separate CL is worth the effort at this point. But if you still prefer that then I\u0027ll do it. I would however do it by keeping the merge functionality in the first CL and add back the else clause at line 51 (in PS2) for property structs in the second, because as argued above that is the simplest and cleanest spot to do the type dispatch. Doing the conversion from property struct to bpPropertySet separately first essentially means moving up the type dispatch to a higher level, which just duplicates code, and also adds code to construct the separate bpPropertySet recursively.\n\n\u003e struct -\u003e BpPropertySet needs to handle fields and nested structs and slices but BpPropertySet merging needs to handle BpPropertySets\n\nWhen the property set to merge into is known, these differences reduce to two different ways of visiting the fields, nothing more. The recursion is the same, etc.\n\nAlso, I haven\u0027t changed anything when it comes to slices. I didn\u0027t test the existing functionality in the new unit tests - I\u0027ll add that.",
      "parentUuid": "1aa130f7_4671e0ce",
      "revId": "e5bf895264f4551430e9e33b4d738d80d75559ff",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
